  "Question": "If one minus the reciprocal of \\((1-x)\\) equals the reciprocal of \\((1-x),\\) then \\(x\\) equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-2\\)",
    "B": "\\(-1\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\frac12\\)",
    "D": "\\(2\\)",
    "E": "\\(3\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The reciprocal of \\(1-x\\) is \\(1/(1-x)\\), so we require \\(x\\ne1\\). The given statement becomes\n\\[\n1-\\frac1{1-x}=\\frac1{1-x}.\n\\]\nSince \\(1-x\\ne0\\), multiply both sides by \\(1-x\\):\n\\[\n(1-x)-1=1,\n\\qquad -x=1,\n\\qquad x=-1.\n\\]\nFor \\(x=-1\\), the original equation reads \\(1-\\frac12=\\frac12\\), so this value is valid. The answer is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
